%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Plugin Reference",
    title: "HamlPlugin",
    summary: "HamlPlugin turns `.haml` files into components for your rendering framework."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Configuration" }
    :markdown
      Configure the component class, element factory, children, variables, and translations:

      ```ruby
      Klenod::Build::Plugins::HamlPlugin.new(
        component_base_class: "Example::Framework::Component",
        factory: "Example::Framework::H",
        component_children: :lazy,
        variables: {
          global: "@__props",
          class: "Example::Framework::Context.current",
          instance: "@__state"
        },
        i18n: {
          class: "Example::Framework::Translator",
          constant: "I18n"
        },
        cache_static_subtrees: false
      )
      ```

  %DocsSection{ title: "Options" }
    :markdown
      - `component_base_class` sets the superclass of each generated component.
      - `factory` receives every HTML tag and component call.
      - `component_children` controls how component children are passed. Use `:eager` for positional arguments or `:lazy` for a block.
      - `variables` maps Haml variables to framework-owned objects. It accepts `global`, `class`, and `instance`.
      - `i18n` configures translations. `class` creates the helper. `constant` names it.
      - `cache_static_subtrees` reuses frozen element trees that contain no dynamic values. This is an experimental optimization.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Variables" }
    :markdown
      Each variable becomes indexed access on its configured receiver:

      ```haml
      %h1= $title
      %p= @@request.path
      %button= @count
      ```

      With the configuration above, these read `(@__props)[:title]`, `(Example::Framework::Context.current)[:request]`, and `(@__state)[:count]`.

      The mappings also apply to assignments. For an indexed state store, reassign mutable values to trigger its assignment method. Use `@items = [*@items, item]` instead of `@items << item`.

      Unconfigured variable kinds keep their normal Ruby behavior. Special globals such as `$!`, `$1`, and `$LOAD_PATH` are never remapped. Neither are underscore-prefixed instance variables such as `@__state`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Component Children" }
    :markdown
      Eager children are positional factory arguments:

      ```ruby
      Example::Framework::H[Card, Example::Framework::H[:p, "Body"], title: "Hello"]
      ```

      Lazy children are passed as a block:

      ```ruby
      Example::Framework::H[Card, title: "Hello"] do
        [Example::Framework::H[:p, "Body"]]
      end
      ```

      Lazy children let components defer work, provide context, or collect slots. The factory should memoize the block if it may read the children more than once.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Components, Ruby, And Filters" }
    :markdown
      Imported constants can be used as tags. For example, `%Button` passes the imported `Button` class to the factory. Custom elements imported through `JavaScriptPlugin` work the same way.

      Top-level `:ruby` defines imports, constants, and methods. Other Ruby blocks run while the component renders.

      `=` prints a value, `-` runs code silently, and printed Ruby blocks such as `= if condition` render their Haml body.

      `:markdown` filters use `MarkdownPlugin`. `:css` filters add inline styles to the module graph.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Companions" }
    :markdown
      A matching `.css` file adds scoped class and tag names through `CSSPlugin`:

      ```text
      components/Card.haml
      components/Card.css
      ```

      Matching translation files are loaded through `IntlPlugin`:

      ```text
      components/Card.intl.en.toml
      components/Card.intl.sv.toml
      ```

      With `i18n`, the plugin creates the configured helper constant:

      ```ruby
      I18n = Example::Framework::Translator.new(self)
      ```

      The example translator provides `I18n.t(...)` and `I18n.lang`. Haml also works without `IntlPlugin` or a translation helper.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Errors" }
    :markdown
      Source maps point runtime errors back to Haml. Parse errors include the original source. Companion and inline CSS keep their own source maps.

